---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StepsStrip from '../components/StepsStrip.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'What makes this different from Compress PDF?',
    a: 'Compress PDF works toward a target you pick and keeps the best quality that fits. This page has no target: it walks to the strong end of the same measured ladder (smaller photo sizes, lower quality) and keeps the smallest result, stopping when a deeper step stops buying real shrinkage.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much smaller does it get?',
    a: 'On a photo-heavy PDF, much smaller than the balanced settings, at a visible cost to the photos. On a text PDF, almost nothing can shrink at any setting: in our tests a plain re-save moved a text PDF from 7,203 bytes to 7,231. The page says so instead of pretending, and returns your original.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will the photos still look good?',
    a: 'They will look softer and smaller, which is the trade this page exists to make. The result line prints the exact photo size and quality used, so the trade is stated, not hidden. If the result is too soft, the target-size page keeps more quality.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where does my PDF go?',
    a: 'Nowhere. It is read into this page and processed in your browser. Watch the network panel while it works if you want to check.',
  },
];
const faqJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
});
const howToJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  name: "How to compress a PDF as small as it will go",
  step: [
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 1, name: "Pick the file", text: "Drop a PDF on the tray, or click to pick one. The file is never uploaded." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 2, name: "Run the strong compression", text: "Press 'Compress, strong'. The photos inside are recompressed at the deep end of the ladder, stepping down until a step stops buying real shrinkage." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 3, name: "Download the PDF", text: "Take away the smallest file this tool could honestly reach, with its exact size and the photo setting printed on the result." },
  ],
});
---

<Base title="Compress PDF, strong: as small as it will go, free · Benchzy" description="The strong end of PDF shrinking: no target, the smallest honest result, with the photo trade printed on the result. Inside your browser; no upload, no account.">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4">
    <section class="mt-8">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-accent-deep">PDF tools · strong mode</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-black mt-2">Compress PDF, strong</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        When the instruction is just "as small as possible": no target, no balancing. This
        page walks the shrink ladder to its strong end and keeps the smallest file it can
        honestly reach. For a size you choose instead, the
        <a class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-deep" href="/compress-pdf/">Compress PDF page</a> works to your number.
      </p>
    </section>

    <StepsStrip who="For a file that must go as small as it honestly can, when there is no target, only a stubborn limit or a slow connection." steps={[{ n: '01', t: 'Pick the file' }, { n: '02', t: 'Run the strong compression' }, { n: '03', t: 'Download the PDF' }]} />

    <section class="ticket mt-6 p-5 sm:p-7" id="cp-tool" data-mode="strong">
      <div id="cp-tray" class="tray p-6 text-center cursor-pointer" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Pick a PDF">
        <p class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Drop a PDF on the tray, or click to pick one</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-soft mt-2">PDF · it stays on this device</p>
        <input id="cp-file" type="file" accept="application/pdf,.pdf" class="hidden" />
      </div>
      <p class="file-loaded hidden" data-loaded-for="cp-file" role="status"></p>

      <div class="grid gap-6 mt-6 md:grid-cols-[1fr_320px]">
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">The setting</h2>
          <p class="font-display text-3xl font-black mt-3">Strong</p>
          <p class="text-ink-soft text-sm leading-relaxed mt-3 max-w-md">
            The deep rungs of the same ladder the target page uses: photos rebuilt smaller and
            softer, step by step, while each step still buys real shrinkage. On the big suites
            the strong setting is a paid extra; here it is a page.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">File spec</h2>
          <dl id="cp-spec" class="mt-2">
            <div class="spec-row"><dt>file</dt><dd>none picked yet</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p class="action-note">
            Before you run it: a scanned page is a picture, so squeezed scans come out softer
            to read. And a PDF whose pictures are not JPEGs may barely shrink at all. Both are
            properties of the file, not a fault in the run.
          </p>
          <button id="cp-run" class="btn-primary w-full mt-5" type="button" disabled>Compress, strong</button>
          <p id="cp-status" class="font-mono text-sm mt-4 leading-relaxed" aria-live="polite">Pick a PDF to begin.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="cp-result" class="receipt hidden mt-8">
        <div class="tear pt-5 flex items-center gap-4 flex-wrap">
          <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold">Result</h2>
          <span class="stamp" id="cp-stamp">Done on your device</span>
        </div>
        <p id="cp-achieved" class="mt-3 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl"></p>
        <a id="cp-download" class="btn-primary inline-block no-underline mt-3" href="#" download>Download the PDF</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">How it works</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          Most of a photo-heavy PDF is the photos inside it. This page reads those photos once,
          then rebuilds the PDF at deeper and deeper settings (smaller photo dimensions,
          lower quality) and keeps the smallest result, stopping when a step buys less than
          one percent more. Text and drawings are never touched; only the photos give.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Honest limits</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          Strong trades photo quality for bytes, openly. It cannot shrink what is not photos:
          a text PDF has almost nothing to give, and this page returns it unchanged with the
          reason, instead of dressing a re-save up as compression. We do not claim the
          archival-grade results of the big desktop programs; we show the before and after
          bytes, which is the checkable part.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">A password-protected PDF cannot be opened by this tool. Take its password off first with the <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/unlock-pdf/">unlock tool</a>, then bring the file back.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 mb-4">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4">
        {faq.map((f) => (
          <article class="ticket-soft p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold">{f.q}</h3>
            <p class="text-ink-soft leading-relaxed mt-1">{f.a}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={faqJson}></script>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={howToJson}></script>
  <script>
    import '../scripts/compress-pdf.js';
  </script>
</Base>
